  "comment": "The chain-name vocabulary — the ONE rule that decides what a directory under `chains/` is. The owner is the Rust core (`crates/memphis-paths/src/chain_names.rs`); the TypeScript arm is a projection of it (`src/memory/chain-names.generated.ts`). Both arms check themselves against THIS file: the Rust runner is `crates/memphis-paths/tests/chain_name_contract.rs`, the TypeScript runner is `tests/unit/chain-name-contract.test.ts`. WHY: the rule had four owners and three answers. Measured 2026-11-23 on the operator's host, the runtime counted 11 504 blocks as live memory, the audit counted 11 975, and the live chains hold 11 228 — the gap was one archive of `cases` (276 blocks) counted as if it were the chain itself, because the old native rule asked \"does the name end in `.backup-<digits>`?\" and nothing else. A dated stamp was a live chain; an epoch stamp was not. WHAT: `kind` is `chain`, `archive` or `foreign`; `logical` is the chain a directory holds; `normalized` is the canonical name of a user-supplied reference (trimmed, then aliased — the trim is the caller's convention in both arms). The corpus is fixed on purpose: a corpus that grew with the host's disk would change the artefact every time a chain was rotated, and a gate that changes when nothing changed is a gate nobody reads.",
